  {
    "episode_id": 72718,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72718.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "I remember when I chaired the immigration subcommittee the last time I had the then president of the Southern Baptist Convention. He said that for years we had two signs at the southern border. One sign said no trespassing and the other sign said help want it. And I think there's some truth to it. I mean people came across and filled needs that we had in our economy and agriculture is a very good example. And there was a circularity to it. People would come. They'd go back. Well, after 9-11, the border got tightened up and rightfully so. But people were stranded over on this side of the border. They couldn't go back and forth this before. There was no program that really worked very well for visas. And so people have been here working. We know from some of the analyses that a majority of the undocumented farm workers, for example, have been here more than 10 years. Majority of them have family here. I mean, they live here. And they're not going anywhere. And if they did, it would be a real blow to the ag economy. So it's a good idea to try and deal with it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
