  {
    "episode_id": 72718,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72718.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "I think we lose a lot of things. First, I don't have a whole lot of ag in my district. I have some in the southern part of Santa Clara County. But I went over to the Central Valley several weeks ago. and met with, I mean, a big ag area. And there's enforcement actions going on that are more vigorous than they had been under the prior administration. The enforcement efforts were primarily directed at people who, you know, were criminals. Now it's everybody who's undocumented and the farmers are very alarmed. They're going to lose their workforce and then they will be able to get their crops picked. from the farm workers' point of view, they're in a state of terror. I met people who are so frightened, they won't leave their home Sunday morning to go to church. So if a state of fear that would continue, it's not a healthy thing for families or communities. But the bill itself does something even more significant for farm country. Right now, the H2A program, which is the existing temporary visa program is very bulky to use. I've heard a lot from farmers. It's really difficult. It's not timely. People get there late. Plus, the wages have just been skyrocketing. In some areas of the country last year, the wages went up 23%. So what this bill does is it stabilizes wages. The union and all the employers agreed that wages in the H2A program would be frozen for one whole year. So that is a significant savings for farmers and then",
